<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raja Ravi Varma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1906">1906</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ravi Varma</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> Varma was an Indian painter, born at Kilimanoor palace in the princely state of Travancore, whose works fused European academic art with Indian iconography. Closely related to the royal family of Travancore, he received the title Raja from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a>. He won acclaim after an award at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1873 and took three gold medals at the World's Columbian Exposition in Chicago in 1893. He painted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> deities and scenes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, such as Nala and Damayanti. In 1894 he started a lithographic press in Ghatkopar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, whose affordable oleographs carried his images to ordinary people.</p></div>
